 model system  you might have had a very good  introduction to patient-derived  xenograph model system from eliabroski's  talk but let me cover it little briefly  here so we have covered why we have to  do such a study and  how how is it done the tumor cells from  the cancer patients are subjected to  next-gen sequencing methods and  techniques and the information about  gene expression data and gene mutation  data and epigenetic variation data and  copy number variation data are all  extracted  these data molecularly characterize the  tumors and these tumors samples are then  grafted into immunosuppressed mouse and  administered several cancer drugs these  mouse or mice models are called as  patient-derived xenograft models and  then when these tumor  when these mouse or mice models are  are given different treatment profiles  their tumor response to the respective  drugs are documented and this data  records a tumor's reaction to different  treatments so both the molecular  characterization data and the treatment  response data are available with the uh  with the  with the publication published by  novartis so  such a lot of data such as wealth of  data generates a lot of research  questions and my interest was  specifically to use this data to design  and implement machine learning models to  predict tumors response to different  treatments so what did we find so first  we find that the traditional use of  using a single gene biomarker to predict  tumors response to any given treatment  was not accurate enough at all that you  can see from the from this part of the  graph where the accuracy of the model  increases as it approaches one and  decreases  when it approaches minus one so  traditional model of single gene uh  markers did not accurately predict the  treatment responses of breast cancer to  these multitude of different treatments  but when such a traditional model was uh  sorry when when i used uh instead of one  gene model okay  when i use instead of a single gene  model  but  a multitude of gene models and  supplement it with the transcriptomic  profile data and copy number variation  data and gene mutation data so what i  observe is most of the  what i observe is most of the prediction  accuracies lie on the north side of the  0.5 which is regarded as a very good  accuracy for such a  limited data set so that is also seen in  the distribution of the accuracy across  the treatments which is so scattered  when i use single gene uh mutation data  which is traditionally used to  multi-omics integration data which are  uh for which the machine learning model  was developed so  what more can be done with such a such a  question and such a data so a gene  certain gene set enrichment analysis can  be performed on the most informative  genes that are identified from machine  learning analysis and then this can be  extrapolated to study and identify  specific biomarkers that can predict and  help us understand tumors response to  different treatments and one can also  extend such an analysis to include  patients data from tcga repository and  that would reveal us interesting  insights into performance of the machine  learning models that are trained on  pre-clinical data but tested on  real-world patient data and  hope this example was very informative  and inspiring for you all to generate  interesting research questions to  perform such an analysis coding was an  interesting i mean was an integral part  for me but if one latch or one is  restricted in encoding experience that  should not deter them from asking such  interesting questions and this is where  i think omixlogic platform bridges that  gap by providing the necessary trading  modules and also necessary platform  required to perform such impactful  research and i thank you for thank you  all
 

